mpared to  um  animals monkeys whatever you want to  call it  but  if they would just see us  as god sees us  his creation we're all humans we're all  supposed to be equal  they just don't give us a chance  kedran tells me to truly understand why  racism is such an issue we have to go  back to a bleak time in our history  hundreds of years ago when africans were  forced into slavery across the americas  kedran's home city jacksonville is named  after a u.s president who owned slaves  andrew jackson  [Music]  and just an hour's drive from here you  can see what remains of one of the  largest tobacco plantations in nearby  savannah  here hundreds of slaves from africa were  forced to work  we're meeting local historian dr stan  deaton to get more info  so we're standing on butler island  there would have been hundreds of people  at various times working out in these  rice fields enslaved people  if we're standing here on a hot wet  summer day and this would have been  typical of the kind of  weather that the enslaved people would  have had to endure as they stood out  here there were mosquitoes there were  alligators there were snakes  there'd been several families probably  to one slave cabin  so it was about as bad as you can  imagine  so how old were these slaves working out  here they would have been all ages but  once an enslaved person reached the age  of about eight they came out and began  working  [Music]  plantation owners were forced to end  slavery in 1865.  so actually being here on the actual  plantation it just makes me feel really  just emotional  yeah and to know also your ancestors  could have been here  i feel like slavery still exists but in  a modern way um police brutality even  though it may not be in the field it's  still happening so it definitely played  a big role in today's racism  kidron's right the end of slavery wasn't  the end of the fight for black people to  be treated equally  to make this point kedran used  jacksonville's oldest church to film the  video to the gospel version of i just  want to live  [Music]  it was here in 1865 that freed slaves  had to fight white people to be able to  worship here  so this church was owned by white  plantation owners  and uh  when slavery ended um they blamed black  people from ever going to this church  but then they went to court and a judge  said nope they can go in fact and they  can actually own this church wow so they  gave the church to the black people who  had been banned yeah that's really cool  but that struggle for equality went on  for generations  in the 1950s and 60s black americans  faced violence and arrest as they  demanded better treatment and equal  opportunities  change did come  and in 2008 barack obama was sworn in as  the country's first african-american  president kedron believes that moment in  history has a massive impact on young  black people today  it doesn't matter whether you're black  or white or hispanic or asian or native  american or young or old or rich or poor  able disabled gay or straight you can  make it here in america  kedron plans to use his music to inspire  young people across the world to let  nothing stand in their way of their  dreams for a better life  we have a long way to go in a long  journey ahead of us i don't know when  it's going to happen but  i believe that we can just keep on  growing and growing um until we reach  our goal  i mean wow that was really moving it was  and i think it was super eye-opening and  thought-provoking as well now if you  guys want to see more conversations just  head to first.news forward slash fyi and  click on the link plus there are details  on our website on how you can create an  fyi news club at your school and club  members can even be a guest reporter see  you next time bye  [Music]

